# The Hearth hill above Emberhollow, where the Hearthflame burned for a
# thousand years. Every ending is decided here.

place the hill
  title: The Hearth Hill
  the sky: night
  the mist: thick
  the ground: hill grass, 44 by 44 m
  you come in at 0 15, facing 0

  a hearth bowl called the Hearth at 0 -4, unlit
  Sorrel at 2.2 0.4, facing 20
  12 standing stones around 0 -4, 10 to 11 m away
  a way to the village at 0 18, turned 180
  8 fallen logs between -14 -14 and 14 14
  6 rocks between -16 -16 and 16 16

  when you first arrive
    say: The top of the hill is thick with Grey. In the middle stands the Hearth: a great stone bowl, cold as a gravestone.
    say: Curled at its foot is a small red fox that glows like the last coal in a grate.

  when you look at the Hearth
    say: The Hearth. A thousand years of fire, and tonight, nothing but ash.

  when you talk to Sorrel
    if met sorrel
      Sorrel: Are you ready, lamp-carrier?
    otherwise
      remember met sorrel
      Sorrel: Oh. A lamp-carrier. I'm Sorrel. I was the Hearthflame, once. Now I'm nearly out.
      Sorrel: Every night, someone from the village used to climb the hill with a stick of wood and a thank-you. A fire needs feeding. Then they stopped coming, and I got smaller, and smaller.
    if embers is 3
      Sorrel: You've brought all three! The woods, the river and the peak. I haven't felt this warm in years.
    otherwise
      Sorrel: You've brought embers. Not all three, but enough for a small fire.
    choose
      choice: Take them, Sorrel. Light the Hearth.
        if embers is 3
          Sorrel: Gladly. But promise me one thing first. Promise me the village will climb the hill again.
          choose
            choice if kindness is at least 2: I promise. And I'll be the first one up the hill, every night.
              Sorrel: I believe you. You've been kind all night. I can smell it on you, like woodsmoke.
              light the Hearth
              say: The three embers leap from your hands into the bowl: red, blue and white. The fire that rises is taller than the houses.
              say: Out over the valley the Grey tears like wet paper. Far below, one by one, every lamp in Emberhollow flickers back to life.
              go to morning
            choice: I can't promise for everyone.
              Sorrel: Honest. Then it will be a fire that lasts as long as someone feeds it.
              light the Hearth
              say: The three embers leap into the bowl, and the fire that rises is tall and gold. Far below, the village lamps come back on.
              say: Next winter, and the winter after, you climb the hill each night with a stick of wood. Some nights, others come too.
              the end: A Fire That Lasts
        otherwise
          light the Hearth
          say: The embers catch, small and stubborn. Not the great flame of the old songs, but a fire, and the Grey backs away from it.
          say: All winter, Emberhollow huddles close around its little light. Next year, you tell Mira, you will go further.
          the end: A Small Fire
      choice: What if I give you Mira's lantern, too? The last spark from the village.
        Sorrel: Your spark? It would make me strong again. But a fire needs a keeper. Someone would have to stay up here and tend it.
        choose
          choice: Then I'll stay.
            you lose the old lantern
            light the Hearth
            say: You open the lantern. The spark and the embers rise together, and the Hearth roars into gold.
            say: The village gets its fire back. Mira cries when you tell her you're staying. But every night, someone climbs the hill with a stick of wood, just to sit with you.
            the end: The Keeper of the Hill
          choice: I can't. Not yet.
            Sorrel: Nobody should have to. Talk to me again when you've decided.
      choice: They're mine. I carried them all this way.
        Sorrel: That's true. They're yours. Keep them, and you'll never be cold again.
        choose
          choice: Then I'll keep them.
            say: You close your hands. The embers are warm, and they're yours, and you walk back down the hill alone.
            say: Behind you, the little fox flickers once, and goes out. The Grey rolls down into Emberhollow and stays all winter.
            the end: Into the Grey
          choice: No. That's not who I want to be. Take them.
            add 1 to kindness
            Sorrel: I knew it. Talk to me again when you're ready.
